Backups and restores
administrator-01b-backup-and-restore
The top box names the saved backup directory, says how many archives it holds, and says whether anyone else is signed on. A backup and a restore both run only when every other session has logged out, and LOGIN refuses new ones while they run, so the page lists the other sessions and refuses to show its boxes while they are there. The list is the archives of the directory, newest first: when each was made, how large it is, how many accounts it holds and for which product. The box at the foot shows the manifest of the archive under the cursor, read without unpacking anything.
B backs every account up, A the accounts you name, R restores the archive under the cursor, D saves the directory backups go to. Each shows its command and what it does and runs it only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. A restore replaces every account the archive holds with the account of the same name, so its box lists them and wants the word RESTORE typed. SDSYS is never backed up, and no password or credential is in an archive.
The packing and the placing of accounts are done by SDSYS, which runs elevated; where they cannot be done the verb says why and the page shows its words. F5 reads the directory, the archives and the sessions again.
